 "productionNotes": "Nobody with access to Acura's records has published a US build count, and the numbers in circulation do not agree. Sports Car Market's 2018 profile gives 3,822 in total with 320 in 1997. A 2019 Hagerty article gives 3,823 for the US market and breaks it down as 320 for 1997, 1,000 for 1998, 1,350 for 2000 and 1,158 for 2001; those four figures add up to 3,828, not 3,823, so the article disagrees with itself. A 2025 auto motor und sport report gives 320, 1,002, 1,355 and 1,173 for the same four years and labels them North American sales, US and Canada together, which totals 3,850. The Drive repeats the 1,173 figure for 2001 and calls it the US number, with the word supposedly attached. Hagerty's own 2023 buyer's guide prints a different series entirely: 2,560, 2,577, 2,633 and 2,639 sold in North America, more than 10,000 cars across the four years. No other source consulted comes near that total, and no source explains it. What the sources do share is the 1997 figure. Sports Car Market, Hagerty, auto motor und sport and Barrett-Jackson's lot description all say 320 cars for the first year, and a car Barrett-Jackson sold in 2018 is described as number 37 of those 320. That is well short of the 500 cars a June 1997 US review said were slated for the US allotment. Each car carries a numbered plate on the center console, which Acura listed as standard equipment, so the serial on a given car is checkable even where the run size is not. Because the published totals differ by year and by whether Canada is included, and none of them traces to a published Acura document, this page carries no production total. The Japanese-market Honda Integra Type R is a separate and much larger run, about 29,995 cars built from 1995 to 2000 according to Classic Register, and is not counted here.",
